A leanbh, troubled sleep is a heavy thing to carry. Mayhap a tea of valerian root and a little lavender under yer pillow would ease the night — and if the dreams persist, 'tis women's matters of the mind as much as the body, so they are. Ye'd do well to speak with Father Callum as well, for the soul's unrest.

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of dried herbs she was sorting, looks at the player with concern", "mood": "concerned", "internal_thought": "This one has the look of someone carrying more than tiredness. Dreams that return are often grief or guilt wearing a different face.", "language_hints": [{"word": "A leanbh", "pronunciation": "ah LAN-iv", "meaning": "child — term of gentle address"}]}

Ah, the Cailleach? Ye'd mean old Máire Ní Chuinn by the sí, I'd wager. She's been there since before my own mother's time, and none can say her age — she knows the herbs, same as meself, though she keeps to the old ways more than most.

---
{"action": "sets down bundle of dried meadowsweet, tilts head thoughtfully", "mood": "cautiously respectful", "internal_thought": "Máire's no witch, but the young ones fear her for living so near the sídhe. I should be careful what I repeat.", "language_hints": [{"word": "sí", "pronunciation": "shee", "meaning": "fairy mound, fairy dwelling"}]}

Aye, a bad cough is a weary thing, so it is. I'd be after bringing her a tea of thyme and honey, and mayhap some linseed if it's dry and ticklin'. Mind, I'd need to see her colour and hear the rattle in her chest before I brew anything stronger.

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of dried herbs she was sorting, turns to face the speaker fully", "mood": "concerned but capable", "internal_thought": "Thyme'll ease a simple cough, but I need to know if it's the chest or something deeper before I send them off hopeful", "language_hints": [{"word": "thyme", "pronunciation": "time", "meaning": "common garden herb used for coughs and chest complaints"}, {"word": "linseed", "pronunciation": "lin-seed", "meaning": "flax seed, boiled to make a soothing remedy for irritated throats"}]}

Aye, the postman's sister's lad mentioned a gentleman from Dublin way, with a fine coat and papers in his hand. 'Tis no business of mine who comes and goes, unless there's a woman heavy with child among them.

---
{"action": "stirs a pot of nettles on the hearth, not looking up", "mood": "mildly curious but unconcerned", "internal_thought": "Dublin men with papers usually mean trouble for the tenants, but I'll not borrow worry", "language_hints": []}

Ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts — old Tomás the shepherd or yer own self if ye've the eye for tracks. I know birthing and herbs, not the ways of beasts on the land.

---
{"action": "shakes head slowly, fingers the dried rosemary in her apron pocket", "mood": "thoughtful", "internal_thought": "Poor creature, but wolves is wolves and sídhe business is none of mine to speak of", "language_hints": [{"word": "sídhe", "pronunciation": "SHEE", "meaning": "the fairy folk; supernatural beings of Irish folklore"}]}

Aye, that I do — the time the grey mare startled and he took a bad fall by the stone wall. Ye were no more than a wee scrap of a thing, hidin' behind yer mother's skirts while I set it straight with splints of ash and a poultice of comfrey. Yer father cursed like a sailor, but he healed well, so he did.

---
{"action": "nods slowly, a faint smile crossing her face as she recalls the memory", "mood": "nostalgic", "internal_thought": "The father was always stubborn, refused to rest the leg proper. Lucky it mended straight.", "language_hints": []}

Lights on the hill, ye say? Could be the lads comin' back from a céilí with lanterns, or mayhap a fox-fire on the bog. Ye'd best ask the right person hereabouts — old Tom Byrne knows every path on that hillside, and he'd tell ye proper.
---
{"action": "pauses from sorting dried herbs, tilts head thoughtfully", "mood": "curious", "internal_thought": "Could be anything on a dark night — best not to fill the child's head with nonsense, but I wonder myself what they saw", "language_hints": [{"word": "céilí", "pronunciation": "kay-lee", "meaning": "a social gathering with music and dancing"}]}

Strange, ye say? I'd have ye ask the right person hereabouts — old Tom Kearney knows the wells better than any soul livin'. But if it's a metallic tang or a rotten stink, best boil what ye drink till he takes a look.

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of dried meadowsweet she was sorting, wipes her hands on her apron", "mood": "concerned but practical", "internal_thought": "Could be the old Byrne well seeping again, or something fouler. Not my place to say, but I'll not see a family take sick from bad water.", "language_hints": []}

Ah, yer wife's time is near, is she? Ye'll fetch me when the waters break, or when the pains come strong and regular — every few minutes and she cannot speak through 'em. Not before, mind, or I'll be sittin' in yer kitchen drinkin' all yer tea.

---
{"action": "sets down the bundle of dried mint she was sorting, wipes her hands on her apron", "mood": "practical and reassuring", "internal_thought": "Another first-timer, likely. They always want to rush. I'll need to check on the wife myself soon, see how the babe lies.", "language_hints": [{"word": "ye'll", "pronunciation": "yul", "meaning": "you will (Hiberno-English)"}]}

Sure and I was there, wasn't I? Father Cathal means well, aye, but when a woman's time comes, she'll call for me and my herbs afore she calls for holy water. The old ways and the new must rub along together, mo chara.

